Sure, here's an example of how to generate an automatic table of contents (TOC) in a LaTeX document:

1. Start with the standard LaTeX document structure:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\begin{document}

% Your document content goes here

\end{document}
```

2. Add the `toc` option to the document class to enable the automatic generation of a TOC:

```latex
\documentclass[toc]{article}
```

3. Add the `\tableofcontents` command right after the `\begin{document}` command to generate the TOC:

```latex
\documentclass[toc]{article}

\begin{document}
\tableofcontents

% Your document content goes here

\end{document}
```

4. To include sections, subsections, and other headings in the TOC, you need to use the appropriate sectioning commands:

```latex
\documentclass[toc]{article}

\begin{document}
\tableofcontents

\section{Section 1}
\label{sec:section1}

\subsection{Subsection 1.1}
\label{sec:subsec11}

\subsection{Subsection 1.2}
\label{sec:subsec12}

