```

Of course, the exact appearance of the TOC may vary depending on your document class and styling choices. But the basic idea is the same: use the `toc` option in your document class, add the `\tableofcontents` command, and use the appropriate sectioning commands with `\label` commands to generate an automatically generated TOC.

To display a chessboard in LaTeX based on FEN notation, you can use the `chessboard` package. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Install the `chessboard` package by adding the following line to your document's preamble:

```latex
\usepackage{chessboard}
```

2. Define the FEN notation for your desired chessboard state. FEN notation consists of the following components, separated by spaces:

- The starting position of the pieces (2-8 characters)
- The number of half-moves since the last zero-move position (1 character)
- The current player to move (1 character, 'w' for white, 'b' for black)

For example, the following FEN notation represents a standard starting position for a chess game:

```
rnbkpnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq -
```

3. Use the `ChessBoard` environment provided by the `chessboard` package, passing in the FEN notation as an argument:

```latex
\begin{ChessBoard}{rnbkpnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq -}
  % Your LaTeX content here (optional)
\end{ChessBoard}
```

This will display a chessboard with the specified starting position.

Here's a complete example:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{chessboard}

